# 10. Networking Interview Questions & Answers

## 1. What is a Computer Network?

A **computer network** is a collection of interconnected devices that communicate and exchange data using communication protocols.

### Key components

* **End devices:** computers, phones, servers
* **Network devices:** switches, routers, gateways
* **Protocols:** TCP, UDP, IP, HTTP, DNS
* **Transmission medium:** Ethernet, Wi-Fi, fiber

**Interview answer:**

> A computer network enables devices to communicate and share resources using standardized protocols such as TCP/IP.

---

## 2. What is the OSI Model?

The **OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) model** divides network communication into **7 layers**:

| Layer | Name         | Example            |
| ----- | ------------ | ------------------ |
| 7     | Application  | HTTP, DNS, SMTP    |
| 6     | Presentation | TLS, encoding      |
| 5     | Session      | Session management |
| 4     | Transport    | TCP, UDP           |
| 3     | Network      | IP, ICMP           |
| 2     | Data Link    | Ethernet, ARP      |
| 1     | Physical     | Cable, radio       |

### Data flow

```text
Application
     ↓
Presentation
     ↓
Session
     ↓
Transport
     ↓
Network
     ↓
Data Link
     ↓
Physical
```

**Interview point:**
OSI is primarily a **conceptual/reference model**. Real Internet implementations are generally described using the **TCP/IP model**.

---

## 3. TCP vs UDP — What is the Difference?

| TCP                               | UDP                                                |
| --------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| Connection-oriented               | Connectionless                                     |
| Reliable                          | Best-effort                                        |
| Ordered delivery                  | No ordering guarantee                              |
| Retransmission                    | No built-in retransmission                         |
| Flow control                      | No TCP-style flow control                          |
| Congestion control                | No TCP-style congestion control                    |
| Higher overhead                   | Lower overhead                                     |
| HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 commonly use TCP | DNS, DHCP, real-time applications commonly use UDP |

### Example

**TCP:** file transfer, web connections
**UDP:** DNS queries, VoIP, gaming, streaming use cases

**Important:** Don't say "UDP is always faster." Its lower overhead and lack of reliability mechanisms can reduce latency, but actual performance depends on the application and network.

---

## 4. What is an IP Address?

An **IP address** is a logical address used to identify an interface and enable **Layer 3 addressing and routing**.

### IPv4

```text
192.168.1.10
```

IPv4 uses **32 bits**.

### IPv6

```text
2001:db8::1
```

IPv6 uses **128 bits**.

### Important distinction

```text
MAC Address → Layer 2
IP Address  → Layer 3
Port Number → Layer 4
```

---

## 5. What is a MAC Address?

A **MAC (Media Access Control) address** is a Layer 2 address associated with a network interface and used for communication within a local link.

Example:

```text
00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E
```

### MAC vs IP

```text
MAC → local-link delivery
IP  → logical addressing/routing across networks
```

For example, when your computer communicates with a remote server, the **IP destination can remain the remote server's IP**, while the Layer-2 destination MAC changes at each routed hop.

---

## 6. What is DNS?

**DNS (Domain Name System)** translates domain names into network information, most commonly IP addresses.

Example:

```text
www.example.com
       ↓
DNS Resolution
       ↓
93.184.216.34
```

### Simplified resolution

```text
Client
  ↓
DNS Resolver
  ↓
Root DNS
  ↓
TLD DNS (.com)
  ↓
Authoritative DNS
  ↓
IP Address
```

DNS commonly uses **UDP port 53**, but DNS can also use TCP and other transports depending on the situation.

---

## 7. What Happens When You Enter `https://example.com`?

This is one of the **highest-value networking interview questions**.

Simplified sequence:

```text
Browser
  ↓
DNS Resolution
  ↓
Get Server IP
  ↓
TCP Connection
  ↓
TLS Handshake
  ↓
HTTP Request
  ↓
Server
  ↓
HTTP Response
  ↓
Browser renders response
```

For HTTP/2 over TCP, TCP establishment occurs before TLS. With HTTP/3, HTTP runs over **QUIC**, which uses UDP as its transport.

